{#
    The board's styling.

    🚨 Emitted only on Picks' own pages — the module gates this hook on the
    path. Convoro has no way for an extension to ship a stylesheet, so a
    `head.after` hook is the door; OnAir, Quests, SEO, Signal and Member Map all
    found the same wall and took it.

    🚨 Every colour is a core token. Hard-coding one would look right in the
    theme it was written against and wrong in every other, and a pick'em is
    exactly the sort of page a community re-skins.
#}
<style>
.picks-meta { color: var(--text-muted, #6b7280); font-size: .875rem; }
.picks-week-nav { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
.picks-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem; }
.picks-game { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .75rem; }
/*
 * 🚨 The two teams sit SIDE BY SIDE, always — `nowrap` is load-bearing.
 *
 * This wrapped before, with each side given a 12rem basis, so on any card
 * narrower than about 25rem the two teams fell onto separate lines and a
 * fixture read as two stacked rows rather than as one game. That is most cards
 * on most screens. A matchup is a pair, and it has to look like one.
 */
.picks-teams { display: flex; align-items: stretch; gap: .75rem; flex-wrap: nowrap; }

/*
 * `flex: 1 1 0` with `min-width: 0` rather than a 12rem basis: the two share
 * the card evenly and are allowed to SHRINK, which is what keeps them on one
 * line without the board ever scrolling sideways — a board that scrolls
 * horizontally is a board nobody picks on. Without `min-width: 0` a long team
 * name sets a floor on the flex item and pushes the card wide anyway.
 */
.picks-side { flex: 1 1 0; min-width: 0; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; padding: .625rem .75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border, #e5e7eb); border-radius: var(--radius, 8px); background: var(--bg-alt, transparent); }
.picks-side-chosen { border-color: var(--primary, #2563eb); border-width: 2px; }

/*
 * 🚨 The pickable tile is a <button>, so every browser default has to come off
 * — font, background and alignment are not inherited into a button the way they
 * are into a div, and without this the two states of the same tile look like
 * two different components.
 */
.picks-side-pick {
    font: inherit;
    color: inherit;
    text-align: left;
    background: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    width: 100%;
    transition: border-color .12s ease, transform .12s ease;
}
.picks-side-pick:hover { border-color: var(--primary, #2563eb); }
.picks-side-pick:active { transform: translateY(1px); }

/* 🚨 A visible focus ring, because this is now the primary control on the page
   and it must be reachable and obvious from the keyboard. */
.picks-side-pick:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--primary, #2563eb);
    outline-offset: 2px;
}

.picks-confidence { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; margin-bottom: .5rem; }
.picks-form-teams { display: block; }

.picks-side-correct { border-color: var(--success, #16a34a); border-width: 2px; }
.picks-side-wrong { border-color: var(--danger, #dc2626); border-width: 2px; }
/*
 * 🚨 `flex: none` so the crest keeps its full size while the NAME is what
 * gives way in a narrow tile. Without it the logo is the thing flex shrinks,
 * and the bigger size below would quietly become the old one again on exactly
 * the cards where it was too small to begin with.
 *
 * No background, no plate, no radius: these crests are not square and most have
 * transparent ground, so anything behind one takes a bite out of somebody's
 * team.
 */
.picks-logo { width: 3.25rem; height: 3.25rem; object-fit: contain; flex: none; }

/* `min-width: 0` lets a long name wrap instead of setting a floor on the tile. */
.picks-name { font-weight: 600; min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
.picks-score { margin-left: auto; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; font-weight: 700; }

/*
 * On a phone the two teams STAY side by side — that is the whole point of the
 * change — and it is the inside of each tile that stacks instead, crest above
 * name. A row layout at this width would leave a 3.25rem crest beside about
 * four characters of team name.
 */
@media (max-width: 32rem) {
    .picks-side { flex-direction: column; align-items: center; text-align: center; gap: .35rem; }
    .picks-score { margin-left: 0; }
}
.picks-form { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
.picks-others { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .375rem; }
.picks-other { font-size: .8125rem; padding: .125rem .5rem; border-radius: 999px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border, #e5e7eb); }
/*
 * 🚨 The table scrolls inside this, not the page. Core ships no front-end
 * table wrapper (`.admin-table-wrap` is an admin class), and a seven-column
 * leaderboard on a phone would otherwise make the whole document scroll
 * sideways — which breaks every other element on it too.
 */
.picks-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
.picks-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
.picks-table th, .picks-table td { padding: .5rem .625rem; text-align: left;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border, #e5e7eb); }
.picks-table .picks-num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.picks-you { font-weight: 700; }
.picks-cards { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem; }
.picks-card { flex: 1 1 12rem; }
.picks-card-value { font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
</style>
